Subject: [PATCH] clk: ingenic: x1600: measure how long cpu set_rate holds IRQs off
Upstream-Status: Pending

Diagnostics for catplay-firmware issue #1.

x1600_cpu_set_rate() (added by
0033-clk-ingenic-x1600-couple-cpu-and-l2-dividers.patch) takes cgu->lock
with interrupts disabled and then busy-polls CLOCKSTATUS for the divider
change to complete, with a 100ms timeout:

	spin_lock_irqsave(&cgu->lock, flags);
	...
	ret = readl_poll_timeout_atomic(cgu->base + CGU_REG_CLOCKSTATUS,
					status,
					!(status & CLOCKSTATUS_CPU_BUSY),
					0, 100 * USEC_PER_MSEC);
	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&cgu->lock, flags);

v0.2.0 of catplay-firmware had no cpufreq at all; v0.3.0 enabled it with
the ondemand governor (691eef1) and this hang fix (19925cf), and v0.3.0 is
where the NAC Wave 2 head unit stopped working. On a running dongle
cpufreq stats show ~12 transitions per second under ondemand, so the
typical stall is clearly short - but the tail is unmeasured, and the tail
is what matters to anything latency-bound: a head unit waiting on a USB
control transfer response does not care about the mean.

Directly measuring the role switch showed it is not affected: it takes the
same 201ms at 184MHz and at 1104MHz, because it is two 100ms IDDIG debounce
waits in hardware, i.e. timer-bound. That test says nothing about the
latency-bound paths, and this kernel has no ftrace (CONFIG_FTRACE is off)
so there is no other way to see an IRQs-off stall.

So measure it here. Timestamp with local_clock() immediately after the
lock is taken and immediately before it is released, and report:

 - every new maximum, as a pr_warn with the requested rate, the stall in
   microseconds, the transition number and the poll result. This is self-
   limiting: it goes quiet once the worst case has been seen.

 - a pr_info summary every 1024 transitions (~90s under ondemand) with the
   count, the max and the mean.

The counters are plain statics updated outside the lock. This is a UP
system and a lost increment in the summary is harmless.

Diagnostics only - no behavioural change to the clock code itself. Depends
on patch 0033.

---
 drivers/clk/ingenic/x1600-cgu.c |  40 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+), 0 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/clk/ingenic/x1600-cgu.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/ingenic/x1600-cgu.c
@@ -12,8 +12,10 @@
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
+#include <linux/math64.h>
 #include <linux/rational.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/sched/clock.h>
 
 #include <dt-bindings/clock/ingenic,x1600-cgu.h>
 
@@ -92,12 +94,30 @@
 	return DIV_ROUND_UP(*parent_rate, div);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Diagnostics: x1600_cpu_set_rate() keeps interrupts disabled while it polls
+ * CLOCKSTATUS for the divider change to complete, for up to 100ms. cpufreq
+ * calls it around a dozen times a second under ondemand, so the typical case
+ * must be short - but the tail is what matters to anything latency-bound,
+ * such as answering a USB control transfer. Track it. Only new maxima and a
+ * periodic summary are printed, so this stays quiet once it has converged.
+ *
+ * The counters are touched outside the lock on a UP system; a lost increment
+ * in the summary count is harmless and not worth a lock.
+ */
+#define X1600_CPU_SET_RATE_SUMMARY_EVERY	1024
+
+static u64 x1600_cpu_set_rate_max_ns;
+static u64 x1600_cpu_set_rate_total_ns;
+static unsigned long x1600_cpu_set_rate_count;
+
 static int x1600_cpu_set_rate(struct clk_hw *hw, unsigned long req_rate,
 			      unsigned long parent_rate)
 {
 	unsigned long flags;
 	unsigned int cpu_div, l2_div;
 	u32 val, status;
+	u64 t0, stall_ns;
 	int ret;
 
 	cpu_div = DIV_ROUND_UP(parent_rate, req_rate);
@@ -110,6 +130,7 @@
 		l2_div = min(cpu_div * 2, 16U);
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&cgu->lock, flags);
+	t0 = local_clock();
 
 	val = readl(cgu->base + CGU_REG_CLOCKCONTROL);
 	val &= ~CLOCKCONTROL_CHANGE_ENABLE;
@@ -128,8 +149,27 @@
 					!(status & CLOCKSTATUS_CPU_BUSY),
 					0, 100 * USEC_PER_MSEC);
 
+	stall_ns = local_clock() - t0;
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&cgu->lock, flags);
 
+	x1600_cpu_set_rate_count++;
+	x1600_cpu_set_rate_total_ns += stall_ns;
+
+	if (stall_ns > x1600_cpu_set_rate_max_ns) {
+		x1600_cpu_set_rate_max_ns = stall_ns;
+		pr_warn("x1600-cgu: cpu set_rate -> %lu Hz held IRQs off for %llu us (new max, transition #%lu, ret=%d)\n",
+			req_rate, div_u64(stall_ns, NSEC_PER_USEC),
+			x1600_cpu_set_rate_count, ret);
+	}
+
+	if (!(x1600_cpu_set_rate_count % X1600_CPU_SET_RATE_SUMMARY_EVERY))
+		pr_info("x1600-cgu: cpu set_rate: %lu transitions, IRQs-off max %llu us, mean %llu us\n",
+			x1600_cpu_set_rate_count,
+			div_u64(x1600_cpu_set_rate_max_ns, NSEC_PER_USEC),
+			div_u64(div_u64(x1600_cpu_set_rate_total_ns,
+					x1600_cpu_set_rate_count),
+				NSEC_PER_USEC));
+
 	return ret;
 }
 
